1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,877
A loose button comes off with one tug.

2
00:00:02,877 --> 00:00:05,017
A stitched one needs a hard pull.

3
00:00:05,017 --> 00:00:08,727
A weak magnet slides off the
fridge, a strong one will not.

4
00:00:09,127 --> 00:00:10,984
Some things let go easily.

5
00:00:10,984 --> 00:00:12,430
Some hold on tight.

6
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:16,168
Aarav reads the lab chart
in the college corridor.

7
00:00:16,168 --> 00:00:19,455
Sodium reacts so fast it
is stored under kerosene.

8
00:00:19,805 --> 00:00:24,107
Neon, just one place before it in
the table, hardly reacts at all.

9
00:00:24,607 --> 00:00:29,161
By the end of this video, you will know
exactly how tightly each atom holds

10
00:00:29,161 --> 00:00:30,471
its outer electron.

11
00:00:31,121 --> 00:00:32,997
So how do we measure that grip?

12
00:00:33,347 --> 00:00:37,412
We pull one electron off an atom,
and measure the energy it takes.

13
00:00:37,761 --> 00:00:42,426
Ionization enthalpy is the energy
needed to remove an electron from an

14
00:00:42,426 --> 00:00:45,397
isolated gas atom, in its ground state.

15
00:00:45,397 --> 00:00:48,413
Isolated means alone, with no neighbours.

16
00:00:48,763 --> 00:00:51,330
It is measured in kilojoules per mole.

17
00:00:51,330 --> 00:00:54,926
It is always positive,
because we must put energy in.

18
00:00:55,526 --> 00:00:57,871
Let's do it on our molecule bench.

19
00:00:57,871 --> 00:00:59,540
Here is a sodium atom.

20
00:00:59,540 --> 00:01:00,985
Two, eight, one.

21
00:01:01,335 --> 00:01:05,828
Its outer electron sits alone in
shell three, far from the nucleus.

22
00:01:06,228 --> 00:01:07,743
Now we pull it away.

23
00:01:07,743 --> 00:01:10,812
The energy bar fills up
as the electron leaves.

24
00:01:11,212 --> 00:01:14,263
Four hundred ninety six
kilojoules per mole.

25
00:01:14,263 --> 00:01:16,443
Sodium becomes a positive ion.

26
00:01:16,843 --> 00:01:19,382
Pulling a second electron is much harder.

27
00:01:19,382 --> 00:01:23,817
It must come from a full inner shell,
closer to the nucleus, and the ion

28
00:01:23,817 --> 00:01:25,152
is already positive.

29
00:01:25,802 --> 00:01:29,688
Now measure every atom in period
two, from lithium to neon.

30
00:01:30,088 --> 00:01:32,094
Lithium, five hundred twenty.

31
00:01:32,094 --> 00:01:34,099
Beryllium, eight ninety nine.

32
00:01:34,099 --> 00:01:35,908
Boron, eight hundred one.

33
00:01:36,308 --> 00:01:38,128
Carbon, ten eighty six.

34
00:01:38,128 --> 00:01:40,104
Nitrogen, fourteen oh two.

35
00:01:40,104 --> 00:01:42,017
Oxygen, thirteen fourteen.

36
00:01:42,417 --> 00:01:44,344
Fluorine, sixteen eighty one.

37
00:01:44,344 --> 00:01:46,129
Neon, two thousand eighty.

38
00:01:46,129 --> 00:01:48,697
The grip gets stronger across the period.

39
00:01:49,347 --> 00:01:50,734
Why does it rise?

40
00:01:50,734 --> 00:01:51,852
Step by step.

41
00:01:52,202 --> 00:01:53,102
Step one.

42
00:01:52,993 --> 00:01:56,957
Across the period, the outer
electrons stay in the same shell.

43
00:01:57,707 --> 00:01:58,607
Step two.

44
00:01:58,552 --> 00:02:01,736
Each step adds one more
proton to the nucleus.

45
00:02:02,486 --> 00:02:03,386
Step three.

46
00:02:03,381 --> 00:02:07,128
So the outer electrons are
pulled harder, and held closer.

47
00:02:07,878 --> 00:02:08,778
Step four.

48
00:02:08,737 --> 00:02:11,333
More energy is needed to pull one off.

49
00:02:11,333 --> 00:02:15,913
And down a group it falls, from lithium
five twenty to sodium four ninety

50
00:02:15,913 --> 00:02:18,806
six, because a new shell sits farther out.

51
00:02:19,556 --> 00:02:20,943
But two bars dip.

52
00:02:20,943 --> 00:02:23,067
Boron is lower than beryllium.

53
00:02:23,067 --> 00:02:27,934
Its electron sits in a two p orbital,
which is shielded more by the inner

54
00:02:27,934 --> 00:02:29,529
electrons than two s.

55
00:02:29,529 --> 00:02:33,620
Shielding means inner electrons
block the pull of the nucleus.

56
00:02:34,020 --> 00:02:37,486
Now compare nitrogen
and oxygen side by side.

57
00:02:37,486 --> 00:02:41,847
Nitrogen has three two p
electrons, one in each orbital.

58
00:02:42,247 --> 00:02:43,359
Oxygen has four.

59
00:02:43,359 --> 00:02:47,407
Two of them must share one orbital,
and they push each other apart.

60
00:02:47,807 --> 00:02:49,163
Pause and think.

61
00:02:49,163 --> 00:02:53,229
Which loses an electron more
easily, nitrogen or oxygen?

62
00:02:56,429 --> 00:02:57,329
Oxygen!

63
00:02:57,104 --> 00:03:01,891
Its paired electron is pushed by its
partner, so it leaves more easily.

64
00:03:01,891 --> 00:03:05,040
Thirteen fourteen is less
than fourteen oh two.

65
00:03:05,690 --> 00:03:06,855
Remember this.

66
00:03:06,855 --> 00:03:10,424
A stronger pull means a
bigger ionization enthalpy.

67
00:03:11,124 --> 00:03:12,860
Now, one worked example.

68
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:17,983
An electron in the ground state of
hydrogen has energy minus two point

69
00:03:17,983 --> 00:03:21,592
one eight times ten to
the minus eighteen joules.

70
00:03:21,592 --> 00:03:24,555
Find the ionization enthalpy of hydrogen.

71
00:03:25,055 --> 00:03:28,981
To remove the electron, we
must raise its energy to zero.

72
00:03:28,981 --> 00:03:33,445
So each atom needs two point one
eight times ten to the minus eighteen

73
00:03:33,445 --> 00:03:34,345
joules.

74
00:03:34,866 --> 00:03:40,092
One mole has six point zero two two
times ten to the twenty three atoms.

75
00:03:40,092 --> 00:03:41,159
So multiply.

76
00:03:41,909 --> 00:03:46,093
That gives one point three one times
ten to the six joules per mole.

77
00:03:46,843 --> 00:03:50,878
That is about thirteen hundred
and thirteen kilojoules per mole.

78
00:03:50,878 --> 00:03:53,936
Hydrogen holds its single
electron very tightly.

79
00:03:54,586 --> 00:03:56,052
Now, the exam trap.

80
00:03:56,402 --> 00:04:01,331
Some students say oxygen has more
protons than nitrogen, so its ionization

81
00:04:01,331 --> 00:04:03,154
enthalpy must be higher.

82
00:04:03,554 --> 00:04:06,442
But oxygen's paired two p electrons repel.

83
00:04:06,442 --> 00:04:11,271
Nitrogen, fourteen oh two, is higher
than oxygen, thirteen fourteen.

84
00:04:11,921 --> 00:04:13,615
Where is this asked?

85
00:04:13,615 --> 00:04:18,707
JEE Main and NEET both test the order
of ionization enthalpies, and the

86
00:04:18,707 --> 00:04:20,514
boron and oxygen dips.

87
00:04:21,114 --> 00:04:22,440
Exam level now.

88
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:27,859
Which is the correct order of first
ionization enthalpy for boron, beryllium,

89
00:04:27,859 --> 00:04:30,096
carbon, nitrogen and oxygen?

90
00:04:30,096 --> 00:04:31,479
Pause and solve.

91
00:04:36,879 --> 00:04:38,304
The answer is A.

92
00:04:38,304 --> 00:04:41,728
Boron, beryllium,
carbon, oxygen, nitrogen.

93
00:04:42,228 --> 00:04:44,772
The values rise across the period.

94
00:04:44,772 --> 00:04:47,426
Carbon is above beryllium and boron.

95
00:04:47,926 --> 00:04:52,586
Boron is below beryllium, because
its two p electron is shielded more.

96
00:04:53,086 --> 00:04:57,105
Oxygen is below nitrogen,
because of its paired electrons.

97
00:04:57,105 --> 00:04:58,961
C missed the oxygen dip.

98
00:04:59,561 --> 00:05:01,022
Here is your recap.

99
00:05:01,022 --> 00:05:02,523
Screenshot this card.

100
00:05:05,723 --> 00:05:08,722
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

101
00:05:09,122 --> 00:05:10,022
One.

102
00:05:09,734 --> 00:05:13,613
Why does ionization enthalpy
rise across a period?

103
00:05:14,413 --> 00:05:15,313
Two.

104
00:05:14,984 --> 00:05:18,087
Why is boron's value
lower than beryllium's?

105
00:05:18,887 --> 00:05:19,787
Three.

106
00:05:19,536 --> 00:05:24,193
Why is the second ionization enthalpy
of sodium so much higher than the

107
00:05:24,193 --> 00:05:25,093
first?

108
00:05:25,742 --> 00:05:30,272
Next time: why does chlorine release
more energy than fluorine when it

109
00:05:30,272 --> 00:05:31,650
grabs an electron?

110
00:05:31,650 --> 00:05:34,314
We find out in electron gain enthalpy.
